 In the route-finding scenes, the model first spreads costs outward from a target through a grid. It then assigns directions towards neighbouring cells with lower costs. Each moving agent reads the direction at its present position and steers towards it. Steering has a precise meaning here. The agent compares the direction it has read with the velocity it already has, and pushes on the difference. A pull like gravity never asks how a body is moving; this rule asks first, and the answer is in the push. That comparison, made inside the mover, is what the word agent adds to the word particle. The expensive task of organising a route is shared through the field; it is not solved afresh by every agent at every step.
 
-Wind makes the distinction sharper. A field can give a direction without giving a destination. The route scenes are path finding: the field is a solved problem, flooded outward from a destination. The wind and noise scenes are field following, with no problem solved at all. The agents behave the same in both; only the field knows which it is. Noise supplies local variation without the same coherent route. Where the direction is zero, the agents have a wandering behaviour rather than a magical knowledge of where to go. That wandering is a new random direction every frame, smoothed a little by the same steering, which is why an agent at the target buzzes rather than roams. Reynolds's own wanderers keep most of yesterday's heading and add a little chance to it; the beads in the Randomness terrarium will show the difference between the two again.
